# Vulnerabilities the eval gates accept on purpose: `make scan-deps` (trivy and
# osv-scanner over the lock and the nested requirements files) and the image gate,
# `make scan-image-<family>` / `scan-image-cpu-<family>`, which publish-image-* runs.
#
# One advisory id per line, `#` starts a comment:
#
#   <id> exp:<YYYY-MM-DD> pkg:<type>/<name>@<version> [pkg:... ...]
#
# - Aliases. Every alias of an advisory is listed (CVE, GHSA, PYSEC), because trivy
#   reports by CVE or, when an advisory has none, by GHSA, while osv-scanner and quay's
#   scanner name the same advisory by GHSA or PYSEC. The aliases were taken from the
#   advisory records at osv.dev; do not derive one id from another.
# - Expiry. exp: is trivy's expiry: from that date the line no longer applies and both
#   gates fail again, so the entry is re-reviewed (2027-05-31 unless the entry says
#   otherwise).
# - Scope. pkg: names the packages, by package URL with name and version, that the id is
#   accepted in, and the gates accept it nowhere else: the same id in another package,
#   or another version of the same one, fails. osv-gate.py
#   (steps/train/distill/images/) reads this file as it is. trivy gets the YAML ignore
#   file scripts/trivy-ignore.py writes from it at gate time (its `purls` field, with
#   names as trivy writes them). trivy cannot apply a scope to a finding it has no
#   package URL for, and matches such a finding by id alone (none of the 19 images'
#   findings lacks one today; see scripts/trivy-ignore.py). The
#   scopes were taken from what the gates report in the 19 published images and the
#   lock, so a new version of a listed package needs a new review, not a wider scope.
#   One file serves every family. An entry names packages, not images, so it covers
#   that package and version in every image that installs it; the comment says which
#   images those are.
#
# Every entry also says why the finding is accepted and which issue removes it;
# delete the entry when that issue lands.

